 bearer token; a 401/403 is a FAILED validation, not an environment excuse.'","infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:397-399: 'Auth ON at /mcp (static bearer)' / REBAR_MCP_AUTH_STRATEGIES: \"static\"","docs/mcp-auth.md:232-235: 'authenticated by the `static` verifier with one bearer PAT per client (Copilot, Codex, Claude). No Authorization Server is stood up ... none require OAuth/DCR'","docs/mcp-client-setup.md:8: 'None of the three requires OAuth for this deployment — one static-bearer shape covers all three.'","Endpoint existence/feasibility VERIFIED: plan's own Phase 1 evidence (http=200, 94.5MB served) plus tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_client_configs.py exercising list_tickets over HTTP with a static bearer."],"finding":"The LIVE VALIDATION AC misnames the deployed endpoint's auth as 'the OAuth gate', but the deployment authenticates with a per-client STATIC bearer PAT, not an OAuth/authorization-server flow — the mechanism named in the auth precondition is contradicted by the deployment config.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fdf7663575ed709ee","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — 'LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE' bullet ('Auth precondition: the endpoint is behind the OAuth gate...')","norm_id":"nb2063b91837178d8","priority":0.3422,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An executor reads 'OAuth gate' and attempts an OAuth/DCR token-exchange (against a non-existent authorization server) instead of exporting the per-client static PAT the endpoint actually authenticates, wasting a validation cycle or wrongly concluding the environment is broken."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Replace 'the endpoint is behind the OAuth gate' with 'the deployed /mcp/ endpoint authenticates with a per-client static bearer PAT (REBAR_MCP_AUTH_STRATEGIES=static; ADR 0104), so the client MUST present its PAT (e.g. MCP_CLIENT_PAT_COPILOT) in the Authorization: Bearer header'; keep the '401/403 is a FAILED validation, not an environment excuse' clause.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7778,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reconcile the refuse-only bound with MCP's standard cursor-based list-pagination utility: state explicitly why refusal (plus narrowing filters / next_batch) is chosen over spec pagination for list_tickets/ready_tickets, and confirm this decision holds even as ready_tickets crosses the budget at its stated ~1.7% headroom.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.8,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["MCP spec (modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/.../server/utilities/pagination) defines cursor-based pagination for list operations that may return large result sets, framed as 'especially important when connecting to external services over the internet'","Plan states ready_tickets 'accepts NO filter argument' and its 'measured headroom after the projection is ~1.7% ... so refusing is a near-term state' — the precise situation pagination addresses, yet the only remedy offered is next_batch(epic_id)","Codebase already exposes a pagination knob: rebar._reads.next_batch(epic_id, *, limit: int = 0) — an internal precedent for bounded list retrieval","Plan text contains no mention of pagination, cursors, or the MCP list-pagination utility (ABSENCE)"],"finding":"The plan commits to a refuse-only response-size bound for two LIST tools (list_tickets, ready_tickets) without considering the MCP specification's standard cursor-based PAGINATION utility, which the spec defines expressly for 'list operations that may return large result sets' — the established prior art for this exact problem.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa7977f1906e41da5","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope / AC 'REFUSE, do not truncate' and 'The remedy must be one the REFUSED TOOL can execute'","norm_id":"n78ace77c30734fc1","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["ready_tickets grows past 90,000 bytes (near-term, per plan's ~1.7% headroom): a refuse-only bound leaves callers with next_batch(epic_id) as the sole escape, whereas spec pagination would return the full set in pages","list_tickets(status=open) that legitimately exceeds the budget after narrowing: refuse gives no way to retrieve the complete set within the client budget"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Add a short prior-art paragraph in Scope citing the MCP pagination utility (and the #2211 block/truncate/off discussion), and justify refuse-not-paginate: e.g. that these tools are agent-facing narrow-and-retry rather than bulk export, that a cursor contract is out of scope for this bound-only ticket, and that the projection prerequisite plus filters/next_batch cover the near-term cases — or, if pagination is the better fit for ready_tickets, split it out as follow-up.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1429,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_o